to-be-incorporated-into-paper-67 = unlines (cons "What remains of the language of hyperactivity between its implementation and the paper: - **Native realization goes through C** (Avik, 2026-11-14). One C emitter runs wherever a C compiler" (cons "  does, a MacBook included; proven emitters per instruction set (x86, Arm, RISC-V) would multiply" (cons "  with every target, and a complete x86 path delivers near-zero value. The paper permits it" (cons (quoted (cons "  (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”, " (cons "Loading, native substitution, and export" (cons ":" nil)))) (cons (quoted (cons "  " (cons "If an external compiler produces the executed bytes, either its relevant compilation relation is" nil))) (cons (quoted (cons "  assumed or those bytes receive their own checked connection" (cons ")." nil))) (cons "  - The emitter returns the relation between running the emitted C and the term's meaning. That" (cons "    needs the meaning of the C fragment the emitter produces, a small fixed subset. The meaning of" (cons "    all of C stays language support." (cons "  - The C compiler's relation is a named assumption, listed by `#trusted`." (cons "  - The evaluator substitutes native code only under that relation" (cons (quoted (cons "    (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”, " (cons "Loading, native substitution, and export" (cons "). Today `#jit` binds x86 bytes with no relation, which breaks this rule." nil)))) (cons "  - The x86 translator (`src/lang.c`'s native path, `lib/x86.cub`, `lib/machine.cub`, `lib/elf.cub`'s" (cons "    pasted machine code, `lib/compile/`) is superseded." nil))))))))))))))
